{
  "summary": "The previous proposal correctly identifies both root causes but its fixes have gaps. (1) For slow initial load: calling smartPollFeatures() immediately on page load is necessary but insufficient — if the Go server has no pipeline client connected yet (or the pipeline hasn't sent its first state_update), the immediate poll just fetches empty state, leaving the user staring at a blank page anyway. The fix should also have the Go server return a loading/skeleton state when it has no data, so the user sees feedback rather than emptiness. (2) For create_idea: the '3 retries at 1s intervals' approach is a non-deterministic hack that will still fail under load or slow pipelines. The better alternative (Go server waits for the next state_update before returning) was mentioned but not explored — it has its own problems: it couples the HTTP response to WebSocket round-trip latency and needs a timeout, and if the pipeline is busy processing other work, the wait could be long. A simpler middle ground: return a provisional/optimistic item from the Go server immediately (the server already has all the data it needs from the POST body), render it client-side as 'pending', and let the next real state_update replace it. (3) The proposal misses that the same race condition affects ALL actions, not just create_idea — move, approve, reject, etc. all use the same pattern of setTimeout(smartPollFeatures, 500), which is an optimistic 500ms poll that frequently loses the race. Any fix should address the general pattern, not just create_idea."
}
